Talk to the storage controller: probe it, then read a sector.

The root filesystem is an 18 MiB RAM disk with 44 files in it, and that is a
ceiling rather than a choice: the shim carries it inside the Android BOOT
partition, which is 32 MiB, and the kernel takes fourteen of those. The real
iOS 12 userland does not fit and never will -- nearly every binary it runs is
resolved out of dyld_shared_cache_arm64, 1.19 GiB on its own. Until the
kernel can read a storage device there is nothing for launchd to start, so
this is the next thing.

The shape it is headed for is a BSD block device, not an IOKit one. An
IOBlockStorageDevice under IOBlockStorageDriver is how a real handset gets
its IOMedia, but IOStorageFamily is a separate kext and is not in this tree,
so that route begins by building another kext into the kernel. It is not
needed. The root device is a dev_t, and bsd/dev/memdev.c -- already patched
here so that rd=md0 works -- is a plain bdevsw with a strategy routine. A
storage driver in that shape needs no IOKit at all; strategy() does real
transfers where memdev does a memcpy.

This patch is the two steps before that, in the order that makes each one
answerable on its own.

    dwmmc_probe=<mask>              read the identity registers (default: 1)
    dwmmc_read=<n> dwmmc_lba=<m>    send CMD17 n times from sector m (default: 0)

First, the identity registers. Bringing up a card means a clock setup, a
reset, CMD0/CMD1/CMD2/CMD3/CMD7, FIFO handling and timeouts, and when the
result of all that is silence there is no way to tell which step was wrong --
or whether the register block was ever mapped. VERID and HCON are read-only.
A plausible value out of them proves the mapping, the bus and the clock
together, in one line, before there is a single card command to be wrong
about.

The addresses are Samsung's own. The stock boot image carries four device
trees, one per board revision, and all four agree:

    dwmmc0@13540000   SPI 245   non-removable, 8-bit, HS400, use-smu
    dwmmc2@13560000   SPI 247   microSD slot, 4-bit, bypass-smu

so this handset's revision does not have to be identified to use them.

Only dwmmc0 is probed by default, and the reason is not caution for its own
sake. S-Boot reads the boot image off the internal eMMC, so dwmmc0 is powered
and clocked by the time this kernel runs. Nothing has brought up the SD
slot's clocks -- Android would have, from the CMU, and Android is not here.
Reading an ungated SFR block on Exynos does not return zero, it hangs the
bus. So dwmmc2 sits behind dwmmc_probe=2, and a boot that stops dead after
naming it has answered the question it was asked.

Second, the read -- and the probe is what made it this small. What came back
was not an idle controller but a working one:

    CLKENA 0x00000001   the card clock is running
    CLKDIV 0x00000001   divided by two, not the 400 kHz of an init
    CTYPE  0x00010000   bit 16: card 0 is on an 8-bit bus
    BLKSIZ 0x00000200   512-byte blocks, already set
    STATUS 0x00000906   bit 8: the card is driving DAT3; FIFO empty, not busy
    CTRL   0x00000010   interrupts on, DMA and IDMAC off -- S-Boot used PIO
    HCON   0x0be42541   bit 23: the hold register is implemented
    VERID  0x5342270a   revision 0x270a, so the FIFO is at 0x200, not 0x100

S-Boot left all of it behind. The card has already been through CMD0, CMD1,
CMD2, CMD3 and CMD7; it is selected and in the transfer state. So this sends
CMD17 and nothing else. READ_SINGLE_BLOCK goes to whichever card is selected,
needs no RCA, and needs to know nothing about what S-Boot did. If the card is
not in fact selected the controller answers with a response timeout, which
gets printed and named; that is a cheap way to be wrong.

Two values from the probe are used rather than assumed: HCON's hold-register
bit decides whether commands carry USE_HOLD_REG, and VERID's revision is what
puts the FIFO at 0x200.

The FIFO is drained while the transfer is running, not after it. It is 64
words deep and a sector is 128, so waiting for the data-transfer-over
interrupt first would stall the controller half way with a full FIFO and look
exactly like a dead card.

That is not a hypothetical. The first version of this read got as far as

    dwmmc:   CMD17 accepted, RESP0 0x00000900
    dwmmc:   data error: RINTSTS 0x00000424 HTO

which is a complete account of what went wrong. RESP0 0x900 is the card's R1
status: CURRENT_STATE 4, tran, and READY_FOR_DATA set -- so the card was
selected and the command path worked end to end. RINTSTS 0x424 is command
done, receive-FIFO data ready, and HTO, data starvation by the host. The data
arrived and nobody came for it, because that printf sat between the command
and the drain, and writing one line to this console means rendering glyphs
into a 720x1280 framebuffer: milliseconds, against a FIFO the card fills in
microseconds.

So command completion and the drain are now a single loop with no printf and
no delay in it -- not even a wait for the controller to accept the command in
front of it -- and everything worth saying is saved and said afterwards. The
data timeout in TMOUT is also set generously before the command rather than
inheriting whatever S-Boot tuned for its own loader, since that register is
what decides when HTO fires.

That fixed the transfer and exposed a second mistake, this one in judgement
rather than in code. The next attempt said:

    dwmmc:   data error: RINTSTS 0x00000424 HTO
    dwmmc:   128 of 128 words had arrived

All the data arrived. HTO was still latched, and this treated it as fatal, so
the sector was thrown away unread. But HTO is not a data error: it means the
host was late to the FIFO. The bits that say the data is wrong are DCRC, SBE
and EBE; DRTO says the card never sent it and FRUN says the FIFO ran under.
None of those fired, and all 128 words were there. A transfer that completes
has completed, whatever the controller made of the host's timing along the
way.

So HTO is out of the fatal mask and printed as a warning instead. That got
one step further and stopped again:

    dwmmc:   all 128 words arrived but the transfer never closed
    dwmmc:   at the end: RINTSTS 0x00000424 HTO

A whole sector, no corruption bits, and no DTO. The missing DTO follows from
HTO rather than being a second fault: once HTO fires the DesignWare core
abandons the data phase, so it never says the transfer is over.

What is left unexplained is why HTO fires at all. Not the host's timing: it
was raised identically whether the drain sat behind a framebuffer printf or
ran in a tight spin, which is a hundredfold difference in latency for no
difference in outcome. Two candidates remain -- the TMOUT write not taking
effect, and the SMU that this controller's device tree says is in its data
path -- and TMOUT is now read back and printed so the first can be settled
from the console.

Meanwhile the read is treated as having succeeded, because by the thing it
was written to test it has: a complete sector with none of the bits that
mean it is wrong. The bytes settle it in a way no status register can.

The bytes settled it:

    dwmmc:   TMOUT reads back 0xffffff40 (wrote 0xffffff40)
    dwmmc:   LBA 1:
    dwmmc:     000  45 46 49 20 45 46 49 20  45 46 49 20 45 46 49 20
    dwmmc:     010  45 46 49 20 45 46 49 20  45 46 49 20 45 46 49 20
    ...
    dwmmc:   CMD17 done: ... STATUS 0x00808d09

45 46 49 20 is "EFI ". LBA 1 was asked for and the first word of the GPT
header came back, so the card is being read and the data path works. But the
same word came back 128 times, TMOUT had taken effect so the timeout was not
the problem, and STATUS reported FIFO_COUNT = (0x808d09 >> 17) & 0x1fff = 64
-- the FIFO completely full and never emptied.

HCON had been printing the answer since the very first probe. Bits 9:7 are
H_DATA_WIDTH: 0 is a 16-bit FIFO, 1 is 32 and 2 is 64. HCON here is
0x0be42541, so bits 9:7 are 0b010 and this core's FIFO is 64 bits wide. A
32-bit read of it returns the low half and does not consume the entry. Hence
one word forever, a FIFO that never drains, a controller that gives up on the
host -- and an HTO that was never about the host's speed at all, which is
why making the drain a hundred times faster changed nothing.

The device tree agrees in hindsight: fifo-depth 64, at 8 bytes an entry, is
512 bytes, exactly one sector.

So the FIFO is read in the width HCON reports. That was necessary and not
sufficient: a single 64-bit load did not consume the entry either, and the
console said so in the same words as before, now with "FIFO 8 bytes wide"
printed beside them.

What it needs is two 32-bit reads: the low half at DWMMC_DATA, then the high
half at DWMMC_DATA + 4. Reading the second half is what consumes the entry.
Reading only the first leaves it in place -- which was the first attempt --
and a 64-bit access is not permitted by this core at all, which was the
second.

This is not peculiar to this handset. Linux carries DW_MMC_QUIRK_FIFO64_32
for precisely this, and the hardware its commit message names is Exynos7870:
the sibling of the Exynos7570 here, same generation, same core. The sector
buffer stays uint64_t so the pair of halves lands aligned.

Three symptoms, one cause, and all of them visible from the first probe if
HCON had been read properly: the repeated word, the FIFO that stayed full at
64 entries, and an HTO that never had anything to do with how fast the host
was.

Nothing is written, ever. There is no code here that can modify the card,
which on the phone's own storage is the only responsible way to start. The
controller is not reset either: CTRL_RESET would discard the clock, the bus
width and the card selection that all of this depends on, so only the FIFO is
cleared.

The register offsets in the new file are the DesignWare layout, written out
rather than taken from anywhere. They are facts about the hardware, but the
readable descriptions of this controller are in Linux and U-Boot, which are
GPL, and this tree is APSL.

One thing found on the way, recorded here because it decides where the root
volume is going to live: dwmmc0's tree says use-smu and dwmmc2's says
bypass-smu. The SMU is Samsung's inline encryption unit and it sits in the
internal eMMC's data path. Between that, and not having to find a free
partition on the phone's own storage, and being able to rewrite the root
volume with dd instead of a flash cycle, the SD card is the better target --
once the controller has been proven on the one that is already running.

The probe is called from bsd_init() straight after bsd_autoconf(), which is
late enough for printf to reach the console. The call site is deliberately
not next to inittodr(): hfs-filesystem-builtin and memdev-ramdisk-root both
insert there, they both apply after this one, and three patches editing the
same six lines is a merge conflict waiting for the next person.

The read works, so this adds the thing it was for: /dev/mmc0, a block device
the filesystem layer can mount.

    dwmmc_part_lba=<first> dwmmc_part_blocks=<count>    publish the window
    rd=mmc0                                             root on it

It is a bdevsw and not an IOKit storage stack, for the reason given at the
top: IOStorageFamily is a separate kext and is not in this tree, while the
root device is only ever a dev_t. bsd/dev/memdev.c is the same shape and is
already patched here, so this is that shape with CMD17 where memdev has a
bcopy. IOFindBSDRoot() gets a case for rd=mmc0 beside its existing one for
rd=mdX, which exists because a memory device has no IOMedia to match either.

Read-only, and not as a precaution that could be relaxed: there is no code
here that can write to the card. This is the phone's own eMMC with Android's
partitions on it. Nothing is given up by that -- vfs_subr.c:1022 sets
MNT_RDONLY | MNT_ROOTFS on the root mount before trying any filesystem, so
the root is read-only whatever the device could do.

One controller, one request at a time. The transfer is polled, so a second
thread arriving mid-transfer would read another's FIFO; a mutex makes that
impossible rather than merely unlikely. Sectors land in a bounce buffer and
are copied, because the FIFO is read 32 bits at a time and a transfer's
pages are not guaranteed to be aligned for that -- one 512-byte copy against
128 uncached register reads is not worth finding out where that assumption
fails.

The per-read commentary is now behind a flag that only the probe sets. A
filesystem asks for thousands of sectors a second, and a printf in the data
path is the bug this driver was already bitten by once.

The window is two boot arguments rather than a partition table reader. There
is no need for one yet: heimdall print-pit says where the partitions are and
those numbers do not move. On this handset CACHE is 6127616 + 409600 and
SYSTEM is 491520 + 5636096, which is 200 MiB and 2752 MiB -- the first small
enough to flash in seconds for a first root on real storage, the second the
size of the iOS 12 root filesystem.

rd=mmc0 then resolved and the root mount died anyway:

    BSD root: mmc0, major 2, minor 0
    bsd_init: vfs_mountroot
    panic(...): "bdevvp failed: open"

The cause was several layers from the panic. spec_open()'s VBLK path reads
the device's block size, sets it to 512, reads the block count, and then
sets the size back:

    if (VNOP_IOCTL(vp, DKIOCSETBLOCKSIZE, &blksize, FWRITE, ctx))
            error = ENXIO;    /* "If it doesn't set back, we can't recover" */

This answered DKIOCGETBLOCKSIZE, which is what makes that block run at all,
and did not answer DKIOCSETBLOCKSIZE, so the restoring call failed and the
open was refused. A device that reports a block size has to be able to set
one. Both of spec_open()'s calls ask for 512 here, because 512 is what this
reports; anything else is refused rather than quietly accepted.

/dev/mmc0 opened, HFS ran, and it found nothing to mount:

    hfs_ValidateHFSPlusVolumeHeader: unknown Volume Signature : 0

A single dump of the volume header's sector is not enough to say why. An
image that was never written and an image that landed somewhere other than
where it was asked to go both come back as a screen of zeroes, and the two
call for opposite next moves -- one says fix the tool that writes, the other
says fix the address. Guessing between them is what the last several boots
were spent on.

So: dwmmc_scan_lba and dwmmc_scan_blocks, which read a whole range and print
every filesystem signature in it, plus the first sector that is not all
zeroes and a tally of how many are not. Scanning CACHE whole is 409600
sectors and takes under two minutes; it cannot be mistaken for a hang
because progress is printed every 65536 sectors.

What it looks for is HFS+ and HFSX volume headers, GPT headers, and ext4
superblocks -- the last because the partition's previous contents being
still there is itself the answer to where the write went. Headers are
reported with the LBA the volume starts at, not the LBA the header sits at,
since the first is what dwmmc_part_lba wants. The ext4 magic is two bytes
and will occasionally match random data, so hits are capped.

The scan reads through dwmmc_read_block with its commentary off, for the
reason the flag exists: a printf per sector would make this slower by orders
of magnitude and, as this driver already learned once, would be a bug in the
data path rather than merely slow.

The scan answered it, and the answer closed a road:

    dwmmc:   scanning 409600 sectors from LBA 6127616 (200 MiB)
    dwmmc:   ... 65536 sectors so far, 0 with data
    ...
    dwmmc:   scan done: 0 signatures, 0 of 409600 sectors had data

Every sector of CACHE is zero after a flash that reported the upload
successful. The ext4 superblock that was there before is gone, so the erase
lands; nothing replaced it, so the write does not. Raw image and Android
sparse image behave identically, and in both cases the download session
never receives its end-of-session confirmation while a BOOT flash in the
same cable and the same protocol always does. Samsung's download firmware
will erase a filesystem partition for us and will not write an image it did
not produce.

So the root volume goes on a microSD card, which is where the header of this
file said it was going from the first probe, for reasons that have only
accumulated: no SMU in the data path, no partition of the phone's own
storage to find room in, no flash cycle to rewrite it, and now no bootloader
in the way at all -- the card is written with dd from a PC.

That needs the controller brought up, because unlike dwmmc0 it arrives with
nothing done:

    dwmmc0   CTRL 0x00000010  CLKENA 0x00000001  CLKDIV 0x00000001
    dwmmc2   CTRL 0x00000000  CLKENA 0x00000000  CLKDIV 0x00000000

VERID reads 0x5342270a on both, so dwmmc2 is mapped and its bus clock runs;
S-Boot simply never had a reason to configure it. dwmmc_sd_init() is the
standard sequence and nothing more: reset, clock, 74 idle clocks, CMD0,
CMD8, ACMD41 until the card is up, CMD2, CMD3, CMD7, ACMD6 for four bits,
CMD16 for a 512-byte block, then the clock goes up. It is behind dwmmc_sd=1
because dwmmc0 must never be given it -- a CTRL_RESET there would discard
the clock, bus width and card selection that the eMMC path depends on.

The clock is four boot arguments rather than a constant because its input
frequency is not known. The two controllers take separate dividers from the
CMU and S-Boot configured only one of them, so dwmmc0's CLKDIV of 1 says
nothing about dwmmc2. The defaults assume a 100 MHz input -- 125 to
identify at 400 kHz, 2 to read at 25 MHz -- and if CMD0 fails that
assumption is the first thing to move. CLKSEL is Samsung's own register,
outside the DesignWare layout, carrying the sample and drive phase; zero is
its reset value and is what the identification clock wants.

Three outcomes from dwmmc_cmd() rather than two, which is the one piece of
this that is not boilerplate. A card declining to answer is not a fault: it
is how an SD 1.x card replies to CMD8 and how a card still powering up
replies to ACMD41. Folding that into the error case would make a working
card look broken on its first command.

The card answered nothing:

    dwmmc:   CMD8: no answer, treating it as SD 1.x
    dwmmc:   the card never finished powering up (OCR 0x00000000)

CMD0 completing says only that the core accepted a command; CMD0 has no
response, so nothing in that line involves the card. Every command that does
expect an answer timed out.

Three things can produce that, and only one of them is cheap to rule out.
The clock could be outside the 100-400 kHz window identification needs: the
divider here assumes a 100 MHz input and nothing establishes that, because
S-Boot configured dwmmc0 and left this controller alone, and the two take
separate dividers from the CMU. The pins could still be GPIOs rather than
MMC signals. Or the card's supply could be off.

dwmmc_sd=2 settles the first by trying all of them in one boot -- eleven
dividers from 1 to 2000, CMD8 and ACMD41 at each. A divider at which either
answers is the right order of magnitude; silence at all of them means the
clock is not what is wrong, and the answer is in the pin controller or the
regulator.

Writing that turned up a real bug, which is what the second pair of eyes was
for: the FIFO depth is only legible in FIFOTH's reset value, and
dwmmc_sd_prepare() writes FIFOTH. One bring-up never noticed. Twelve in a
row read a depth of 4 from the eleventh onwards. It is latched now.

Every divider was silent, and the STATUS column said why it was not the
clock:

    CLKDIV    8: ... STATUS 0x00000206      command FSM idle
    CLKDIV  125: ... STATUS 0x00000226      FSM 2, sending the start bit
    CLKDIV 1000: ... STATUS 0x00000276      FSM 7, awaiting the response

Bits 7:4 are the command state machine and it is clocked by the card
interface clock. A machine that advances to "transmitting the command" and
then to "waiting for the response start bit" has a clock, has a command, and
is being ignored. The CMU does not need touching.

What was left was the pins, and the device tree is unambiguous:

    sd2-clk            gpr4-0                  function 2
    sd2-cmd            gpr4-1                  function 2
    sd2-bus-width1     gpr4-2                  function 2
    sd2-bus-width4     gpr4-3 gpr4-4 gpr4-5    function 2
    dwmmc2_tflash_en   gpa0-3                  function 1, value 1

S-Boot configures the pins for the eMMC because it boots from it, and leaves
the card slot's as GPIOs. The last line is the half that is easy to miss:
the slot's supply is behind a GPIO that Linux raises before touching the
controller, and nothing here was raising it. The card may have had no power
through every attempt so far, which would explain a silence that no clock
could fix.

The bank offsets are not in the device tree. Exynos7870 is this part's
sibling and its FSYS banks are gpr0 at 0x000, gpr1 at 0x020, gpr2 at 0x040,
gpr3 at 0x060 and gpr4 at 0x080, with six pins in gpr4 and eight in gpr1 --
exactly the six the tree names for the card slot and exactly the eight of
the eMMC's data bus. That agreement is the evidence, and gpr1's function
register is printed as the proof: S-Boot set those eight to function 2, so
it must read 0x22222222 before anything here is believed.

Only the function and the pull are written. The tree asks for a drive
strength of 4 on gpa0-3, which does not fit the two bits a pull field has,
so that field's width is not established and the default is left alone. It
is a centimetre of trace to a socket.

The first run with the pins configured scrolled its own answer away. The
console is a framebuffer with no scrollback and bsd_init() prints some forty
lines after the probe, so the two lines that mattered -- gpr1's function
register and gpr4's after -- were gone before the screen could be
photographed. dwmmc_halt=1 spins once the probe is done.

What did survive placed gpa0. The tree puts uart2 on gpa1-5 and gpa1-6 at
function 3, S-Boot uses that port, and the dump has

    alive +0x060: CON 0x03300100

with nibbles 5 and 6 set to 3. So +0x060 is gpa1 and +0x040 is gpa0, which
was the guess, now evidence.

It also showed the supply GPIO not coming up. Its function register took the
write -- CON read back 0x00001000, pin 3 an output -- and its data register
did not: the bit stayed clear. The controller comes up with a pull-down on
that pin and the tree asks for no pull, so the pull is cleared before the
pin is driven rather than left to argue with it, and the data register is
read back twice, once immediately and once after the supply has had time to
rise. The two reads mean different things: the first says whether the
register took the write at all, the second says what the pad is sitting at,
and on these controllers an output pin's data register can report the pad
rather than the latch.

With the boot halted so nothing scrolled, the pin configuration said plainly
that it had not worked:

    dwmmc:   gpr1 CON 0x22222222 -- the eMMC's eight pins, as expected
    dwmmc:   gpr4 before: CON 0x00000000  PUD 0x00000000
    dwmmc:   gpr4 after:  CON 0x00000000  PUD 0x00000000

The base address is right -- gpr1 reads what only gpr1 can read -- and the
write to gpr4 vanished. Reads work and writes do not, in the same block, at
a different offset: that is an unimplemented register, which reads as zero
and swallows what is written to it.

Exynos7870's bank table was taken over too readily. It has five banks in
this block and this part's tree declares three: gpr0 at line 770, gpr1 at
777, gpr4 at 784, with no gpr2 and no gpr3 anywhere. Three consecutive banks
put gpr4 at 0x040, and 0x080 is past the end of the block.

Both anchors are printed now rather than one, and the whole block beside
them: gpr0's three pins must read 0x00000222 and gpr1's eight 0x22222222,
and the write is read back and called out by name if it does not take.

The supply did come up once its pull was cleared first:

    dwmmc:   card supply after 10 ms: DAT 0x00000098 -- pin 3 is high

and gpa0 is confirmed at 0x040 by gpa1 at 0x060 carrying uart2 on pins 5
and 6 at function 3, which is S-Boot's own console.
--- /dev/null
+++ b/bsd/dev/dwmmc.c
@@ -0,0 +1,1924 @@
+/*
+ * dwmmc.c -- a first look at the DesignWare Mobile Storage Host Controller
+ * on Exynos 7570.
+ *
