+ * the Android BOOT partition, and that partition is 32 MiB. Fourteen of those
+ * megabytes are the kernel, so the root volume has a hard ceiling of 18 MiB
+ * and currently holds 44 files. The real iOS 12 userland does not fit in that
+ * and never will: almost every binary is resolved out of
+ * dyld_shared_cache_arm64, which is 1.19 GiB on its own. Until the kernel can
+ * read a real storage device, there is nothing for launchd to start.
+ *
+ * Why a BSD block device rather than an IOKit one. The obvious shape would be
+ * an IOBlockStorageDevice under IOBlockStorageDriver, which is how a real
+ * device gets its IOMedia. But IOStorageFamily is a separate kext and is not
+ * in this tree, so that road starts with building another kext into the
+ * kernel. It is not needed: the root device is a dev_t, and bsd/dev/memdev.c
+ * -- already patched here to make rd=md0 work -- is a plain bdevsw with a
+ * strategy routine. A storage driver in that shape needs no IOKit at all;
+ * strategy() does real transfers instead of a memcpy. That is where this is
+ * going.
+ *
+ * What this file does today: nothing but read the controller's identity
+ * registers and print them. That is deliberate. Talking to a card means a
+ * clock setup, a reset, the CMD0/CMD1/CMD2/CMD3/CMD7 sequence, FIFO handling
+ * and timeouts, and if the result is silence there is no way to tell which of
+ * those was wrong -- or whether the register block was even mapped. VERID and
+ * HCON answer that one question by themselves: they are read-only identity
+ * registers, so a plausible value proves the mapping, the bus and the
+ * controller's clock in a single line of output, before a single card command
+ * exists to be wrong.
+ *
+ * Where the addresses come from. The stock Samsung boot image carries four
+ * device trees, one per board revision; all four agree, which is what makes
+ * it safe not to care which revision this handset is:
+ *
+ *     dwmmc0@13540000   SPI 245   non-removable, 8-bit, HS400, use-smu
+ *     dwmmc2@13560000   SPI 247   microSD slot, 4-bit, bypass-smu
+ *
+ * dwmmc0 is the internal eMMC and dwmmc2 is the card slot. The probe defaults
+ * to dwmmc0 alone, for a reason worth stating: S-Boot reads the boot image
+ * off the eMMC, so that controller is powered and clocked by the time this
+ * kernel runs. The SD slot may not be -- Android would have brought its
+ * clocks up from the CMU, and nothing has. Reading the registers of an
+ * ungated block on Exynos does not return zero; it hangs the bus. So dwmmc2
+ * is behind dwmmc_probe=2, and a boot that stops dead after asking for it has
+ * answered the question.
+ *
+ * The register offsets below are the DesignWare layout. They are facts about
+ * the hardware and are written out here rather than copied from anywhere:
+ * the readable descriptions of this controller are in Linux and U-Boot, both
+ * GPL, and this file is APSL.
+ *
+ * The SMU in dwmmc0's device tree is Samsung's inline encryption unit. It
+ * sits in the data path of the internal eMMC and will have to be dealt with
+ * before that controller can be read from; the card slot does not have it,
+ * which is one of several reasons the root volume is headed for an SD card
+ * and not a partition of the phone's own storage.
+ *
+ *
+ * Reading a sector, and why there is no card bring-up here
+ *
+ * The probe answered more than it was asked. What came back was not an idle
+ * controller but a working one:
+ *
+ *     CLKENA 0x00000001   the card clock is running
+ *     CLKDIV 0x00000001   divided by two, not the 400 kHz of an init
+ *     CTYPE  0x00010000   bit 16: card 0 is on an 8-bit bus
+ *     BLKSIZ 0x00000200   512-byte blocks, already set
+ *     STATUS 0x00000906   bit 8: the card is driving DAT3; FIFO empty, not busy
+ *     CTRL   0x00000010   interrupts on, DMA and IDMAC off -- S-Boot used PIO
+ *
+ * S-Boot reads the boot image off this eMMC, and it left every bit of that
+ * behind. The card has already been through CMD0, CMD1, CMD2, CMD3 and CMD7;
+ * it is selected and in the transfer state. None of that has to be done
+ * again, and doing it anyway would mean writing the part of a storage driver
+ * that is hardest to debug blind -- clock programming and a state machine
+ * with no output -- in order to arrive back at the state already in the
+ * registers.
+ *
+ * So this sends CMD17 and nothing else. READ_SINGLE_BLOCK is addressed to
+ * whichever card is selected, so it needs no RCA and no knowledge of what
+ * S-Boot did. If the card is not in fact selected the controller answers with
+ * a response timeout, which this prints; that is a cheap way to be wrong.
+ *
+ * Two details from the probe are used rather than assumed. HCON bit 23 is
+ * set, so the hold register exists and commands must carry USE_HOLD_REG.
+ * VERID's revision is 0x270A, which is past 0x240A, so the FIFO is at 0x200
+ * rather than the 0x100 of the older IP.
+ *
+ * The FIFO is drained by polling, not by interrupt. There is no interrupt
+ * handler here and there should not be one yet: a polled read is the version
+ * whose failure modes are all visible in RINTSTS afterwards.
+ *
+ * Nothing is written, ever. This file has no code that can modify the card,
+ * which on the phone's own eMMC is the only responsible way to start.
+ */
+
+#include <sys/param.h>
+#include <sys/systm.h>
+#include <sys/buf.h>
+#include <sys/conf.h>
+#include <sys/disk.h>
+#include <sys/proc.h>
+#include <sys/uio.h>
+#include <mach/vm_types.h>
+#include <kern/locks.h>
+#include <libkern/libkern.h>
+#include <miscfs/devfs/devfs.h>
+#include <pexpert/pexpert.h>
